Listen on the loopback interface only.

Sock_open() (src/modules/internet/sock.c), behind serverSocket() and
socketConnection(server = TRUE), binds to INADDR_ANY, so a listening
socket accepts connections from any host the machine can be reached
from.  The sockets target holds one for the duration of each input, and
accepts whenever a connection it made itself is queued; a connection
arriving from elsewhere in that window would be accepted in its place,
and the target would then read whatever that host sends.  Bind to
127.0.0.1 instead, which is where every connection the target makes
goes.

Instrumentation, not a fix: listening on every interface is what R's
server sockets are for.  Not for upstream.

diff --git a/src/modules/internet/sock.c b/src/modules/internet/sock.c
index a5d799de33..51500214dc 100644
--- a/src/modules/internet/sock.c
+++ b/src/modules/internet/sock.c
@@ -255,7 +255,9 @@ int Sock_open(Sock_port_t port, int blocking, Sock_error_t perr)
 	return Sock_error(perr, R_socket_errno(), 0);
     }
     server.sin_family = AF_INET;
-    server.sin_addr.s_addr = INADDR_ANY;
+    /* Only the fuzzing process itself may connect: a fuzzer listening on
+       every interface would let any host on the network feed it input. */
+    server.sin_addr.s_addr = htonl(INADDR_LOOPBACK);
     server.sin_port = htons((short)port);
 
 #ifndef Win32
